Z||000013||Cheever3000||08-30-2001||07:51 PM||||I'm not aware of any blower or cooling fan in my 88GT's engine compartment.<P><BR>------------------<BR><I>Rancher Chuck</I><BR><A HREF="http://home.houston.rr.com/fieropartsrelay" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Parts Relay</A> administrator<p>[This message has been edited by Cheever3000 (edited 09-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||08-30-2001||08:11 PM||||88's don't have the blowers. None of 'em. <BR>They do, however, have 1/4" of thermal insulation wrapped around the V-6 crossover pipe, where the older ones just had metal shields in place.<BR>I took the wrap off of my first Formula, to repair a crack in the "Y" pipe. Afterwards, I did notice that the engine compartment seemed hotter. It was also after that, that my ignition started getting screwy. Replaced the ignition module and coil, so as not to get stranded.<BR> <BR>I have also heard about some of the blowers on the earlier V-6 cars being wired backwards. I had forgotten about it.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||hyhy4u||08-30-2001||10:11 PM||||Now if i understand you guys right..and i aint no rocket scientist!...but it seems to be you guys are saying that there blowing air out instead of blowing air in correct??  Well if thats to be the case i think its wired right!. Its a proven fact that it will actually stay cooler if you suck air out instead of trying to blow it in..the same thing applies to like a central fan in your house. It sucks air out instead of blowing it in!  Just my thoughts!<P>-Jason||||reg||1
Z||000016||b lo 0||08-30-2001||11:17 PM||||The purpose of the blower fan was not to remove air from the compartment, as in such small quantities as the blower is capable of producing, this would be ineffectual.  That job is left to the decklid vents.  The blower was designed to move fresh air around vital components (coil, alternator) to keep them from getting fried in the greenhouse they call an engine bay.  Mine is totally nonfunctional right now, gotta fix it.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||08-30-2001||11:19 PM||||As I was pointing out the other night to a local forum member(can't remember who it was), I ripped the pipes and that heat shield out of my '86SE 2.8L. They are in the way, and I feel the heat shield on the trunk wall hurts more than it helps, plus no '88 had the cooling fan or trunk mounted shield anyway. I doubt it was a cost cutting measure, Pontiac didn't even bother designing the '88 space for the fan or tubes.<P>The fan in back isn't on all that often anyway, only with the rad fan.||||reg||1
Z||000018||johnmac||08-30-2001||11:25 PM||||WRONG, WRONG, WRONG.<P>The purpose of the duct work is to blow cool outside air across the electronics.<P>If you put your fan on the bench and juice it per the existing wiring as factory installed (black is ground) ---- You will feel nothing as the rotation is sucking the air in and this suction is very slim to say the least. The suction is not feasible over the distance of the installed duct work.<P>If you reverse the wiring you will immediately recognize the difference. A 20 degree reduction in thermals can spell relief for some electronic components.<P>BOTTOM LINE --- LEAVE THE WIRING AS IS AND REPLACE YOUR ELECTRONICS & COIL EVERY 6 MONTHS.<P>TRUST ME - I AM A MECHANICAL ENGINEER AND ALSO DIDN'T BELIEVE IT AT FIRST!!!!!<P>BETTER CHANGE THE WIRING NOW OR YOU BE SORRY!<P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||6
