Z||000023||johnmac||08-31-2001||07:55 AM||||To Galen<P>I am a very mild nice old boy until I see a dumb statement like <P>"Its a proven fact that it will actually stay cooler if you suck air out instead of trying to blow it in..the same thing applies to like a central fan in your house. It sucks air out instead of blowing it in! Just my thoughts!"<P>It was time to wake up the troops! Tha's why I SHOUTED! <P>Let's be honest ---- GM did some sloppy engineering which we need to correct for our lovable Fieros. How many forum members think I am blowing hot air on this subject!<P><P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000024||Avery Montembeault||08-31-2001||11:43 AM||||Sucking hot air out will lower the ambiant temperature of the engine compartment but its spread out over the entire compartment in which air can flow. blowing cold air in will cool in a specific location. if you were cooling just your electronic components, then a directionsl air flow would be best because you could cool it much faster then if you had to suck all the hot air out of the compartment. if the compartment was smaller or the air flow constricted to a discrete location, then direction would be negligable, but that is not the case here. To avoid describing things like thermal gradiant vectors and crap like that let me just say this: set up a box fan in your house. stand in front of it for a minute. then stand behind it for a minute and see which feels cooler...<P>by the way, Im working on my masters degree in physics...||||reg||1
Z||000025||Hartz||08-31-2001||11:49 AM||||It is true that many early cars were wired incorrectly from the factory.  I toured the factory back in '85 and was told by one of the guys on the line that they had wired a number of cooling fans wrong, and that I should go home and check mine.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000026||StuGood||08-31-2001||11:53 AM||||[Sigh] john, john, john....<P>You're right, right, right.  Even if you are an M.E.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> The ducts should <B>blow</B> air onto the electricals.  I'm cool with that.   ||||reg||12
Z||000027||johnmac||08-31-2001||12:04 PM||||To Avery Montembeault,<P>Cool - best explanation to what it is all about.<P>Nice to know that even 'Smart People' love Fieros!<P>Thanks for the input.<P>PS - tried for my Masters but didn't have the time. <P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000028||theogre||08-31-2001||08:19 PM||||There is no way I'd ever remove one of the aux V6 fans. If I had one of those sets I'd add it to my DIS 4 cyl... When I did me short study of engine bay heat, it was only around 70F out side. Can you imagine how much hotter it is at 90? 100? Temperatures nearly the entire U.S. saw this summer... (It was to hot to fool around with measuring it. I did think about it...)<P>Yes, the quick test is to turn on the AC and see if it blows air out the duct. If not then the fan is probably wired wrong, or dead.<P>The easy fix is to alter the motor connection.<P>Cooling the alternator and ignition improves both component life and efficiency. When you figure a traditionally designed alternator is only around 50% efficient to start with, loosing more to heat is bad for both the electronics and engine performance.<P>The 88 had insulation wrap.... Well that was probably cheaper than the fan assembly.<P>Never Ever read the fact that GM deleted the fan as meaning it's not needed. GM and other car makers commonly delete items based only on cost. They don't care if it effects reliability as long as the majority of cars get thru warrantee. Warrantee on Fiero was what? 1 year or 12,000 miles? ||||reg||1
Z||000029||87GTZ34||08-31-2001||09:15 PM||||H-m-m-m-m  Both of my GTs were wired correctly from the factory.  Guess I got lucky with regard to the those bumbling idiot Fiero factory workers.||||reg||1
Z||000030||Fierokid87||08-31-2001||11:46 PM||||Yeah the wiring I got from the GT I have the V-6 out of seems fine too me.  I had the thing just blowing air on the engine when the 4 cyld was in.  so its working for me.  The 88's did not have them which was a mistake not to cuzz My friend PIG has replaced those parts the air helps to cool.  There is a black 85 GT in the bone yard out here i might grab it for an extra fan<P><BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="BLOODY"> Fierokid's  Web site<BR>http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero<P>AIM: Fieroguy1987<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
